// Vitest Snapshot v1, https://vitest.dev/guide/snapshot.html

exports[`render > catalog lists every flow with its description, the skipped pairs and the reusable parts 1`] = `
"# Shelf: flows catalog

## Flows
- F01 Sign up (Reader, auth, starts On the landing page): A new reader creates an account with an email and a password and lands on their empty reading list.
- F02 Sign in (Reader, auth, starts On the landing page): A returning reader signs in with their email and password and sees their reading list.
- F03 Add a book to the reading list (Reader, create Book, starts On the reading list): The reader adds a book with its title, author and shelf; the book appears first in the list.
- F04 Browse and search the shelf (Reader, browse Book, starts On the reading list): The reader browses the list, newest first, and narrows it by typing part of a title or author.
- F05 Delete a book (Reader, delete Book, starts On the reading list): The reader picks a book and deletes it after confirming; the list no longer shows it.
- F06 Sign out (Reader, auth, starts Anywhere in the app, signed in): The reader signs out and sees the landing page.

## Skipped
- Reader, home: The reading list is where a signed-in reader lands; F04 covers it.
- Reader, edit Book: A book is typed once; a wrong entry is deleted and added again.
- Reader, view Book: A book has only a title, an author and a shelf; F05 opens it before deleting it.
- Reader, act Book: The MVP sets a book's shelf when it is added; moving it waits for a later version.
- Reader, account: The reader has only an email and a password, and the MVP has no profile.

## Fields

### Book
- FD03 Title (required, up to 200 characters), e.g. "The Left Hand of Darkness". How the reader refers to the book
- FD04 Author (optional, up to 120 characters), e.g. "Ursula K. Le Guin". Who wrote it, to tell books with similar titles apart
- FD05 Shelf (required, one of: To read, Reading, Finished), e.g. "To read". Where the reader is with the book

### Kept by no record
- FD01 Email (required, up to 254 characters), e.g. "ana@example.com". How the reader signs in and can be reached
- FD02 Password (required, 8 to 128 characters), e.g. "correct-horse-battery". The secret that protects the reader's list

## Forms
- FM01 Book form: FD03, FD04, FD05

## New assumptions
- A03 (presentation, affects F04): Books are listed newest first; there is no manual ordering.
"
`;

exports[`render > flows read as numbered steps, fields with rules, changes, other paths, outcome 1`] = `
"# Shelf: user flows

## F01 Sign up
Reader · auth · Goal: Create an account to keep a private reading list.
Starts: On the landing page
Before: Not signed in.

1. The visitor chooses to sign up. The website shows the sign-up form.
2. The visitor fills in the form and submits it.
   - Email (required, up to 254 characters), e.g. "ana@example.com"
   - Password (required, 8 to 128 characters), e.g. "correct-horse-battery"
   The website creates the account, signs the reader in and shows their start page.

Other paths
- F01.A01 (from 2, invalid) The email is not an address or the password is shorter than 8 characters:
  1. (system) The website marks the field and keeps what was typed.
  Back to 2.
- F01.A02 (from 2, failure) Creating the account fails:
  1. (system) The website keeps the values and offers to try again.
  Back to 2.
- F01.A03 (from 2, conflict) The email already has an account:
  1. (system) The website says so and offers to sign in instead.
  2. The visitor chooses to sign in. The website shows the sign-in form.
  Flow ends.

Outcome: The reader has an account and is signed in; after a refresh they are still signed in.

## F02 Sign in
Reader · auth · Goal: Get back to the reading list.
Starts: On the landing page
Before: Has an account.; Not signed in.

1. The reader chooses to sign in. The website shows the sign-in form.
2. The reader enters their email and password and submits.
   - Email (required, up to 254 characters), e.g. "ana@example.com"
   - Password (required, 8 to 128 characters), e.g. "correct-horse-battery"
   The website signs the reader in and takes them to their reading list.

Other paths
- F02.A01 (from 2, invalid) A field is empty:
  1. (system) The website marks the field and keeps what was typed.
  Back to 2.
- F02.A02 (from 2, denied) The email and password do not match an account:
  1. (system) The website says so and keeps the email typed.
  Back to 2.

Outcome: The reader is signed in and stays signed in after a refresh.

## F03 Add a book to the reading list
Reader · create Book · Goal: Remember a book to read later.
Starts: On the reading list
Before: Signed in.

1. The reader chooses to add a book. The website shows an empty book form.
2. The reader fills in the book and saves it. (Book form)
   - Title (required, up to 200 characters), e.g. "The Left Hand of Darkness"
   - Author (optional, up to 120 characters), e.g. "Ursula K. Le Guin"
   - Shelf (required, one of: To read, Reading, Finished), e.g. "To read"
   The website saves the book and shows it first in the reading list.
   Changes: creates Book.

Other paths
- F03.A01 (from 2, invalid) The title is empty:
  1. (system) The website marks the field and keeps what was typed.
  Back to 2.
- F03.A02 (from 2, failure) Saving fails:
  1. (system) The website keeps the values and offers to retry.
  Back to 2.
- F03.A03 (from 1, cancel) The reader cancels:
  1. (system) Nothing is saved; the website shows the reading list as it was.
  Flow ends.

Outcome: The book is saved and is still there after a refresh.

## F04 Browse and search the shelf
Reader · browse Book · Goal: Find a book on the list.
Starts: On the reading list
Before: Signed in.

1. The reader opens their reading list. The website lists the reader's books, newest first, each with its title, author and shelf.
   Changes: lists Book.
2. The reader types part of a title or an author. The website narrows the list to the books that match as the reader types.
   Changes: lists Book.

Other paths
- F04.A01 (from 1, empty) The reader has no books yet:
  1. (system) The website says the list is empty and offers to add a book.
  Flow ends.
- F04.A02 (from 2, empty) No book matches:
  1. (system) The website says nothing matched and offers to clear the search.
  Back to 1.

Outcome: Nothing changed; the reader has found the book they were after.

## F05 Delete a book
Reader · delete Book · Goal: Remove a book the reader no longer wants on the list.
Starts: On the reading list
Before: Signed in.; At least one book on the list.

1. The reader opens a book from the list. The website shows the book's title, author and shelf.
   Changes: reads Book.
2. The reader chooses Delete on the book. The website asks the reader to confirm deleting the book.
   Changes: deletes Book.

Other paths
- F05.A01 (from 1, notFound) The book was deleted in the meantime:
  1. (system) The website says the book is gone and shows the reading list.
  Flow ends.
- F05.A02 (from 2, confirm) The reader confirmed the deletion:
  1. (system) The website shows the reading list without the book.
  Flow ends.
- F05.A03 (from 2, cancel) The reader cancelled:
  1. (system) The website shows the book again, unchanged.
  Flow ends.

Outcome: The book is gone and stays gone after a refresh.

## F06 Sign out
Reader · auth · Goal: Leave the reading list private on a shared device.
Starts: Anywhere in the app, signed in
Before: Signed in.

1. The reader chooses to sign out. The website signs the reader out and shows the public start page.

Outcome: The reader is signed out; a refresh still shows the public start page.

## Fields

### Book
- FD03 Title (required, up to 200 characters), e.g. "The Left Hand of Darkness". How the reader refers to the book
- FD04 Author (optional, up to 120 characters), e.g. "Ursula K. Le Guin". Who wrote it, to tell books with similar titles apart
- FD05 Shelf (required, one of: To read, Reading, Finished), e.g. "To read". Where the reader is with the book

### Kept by no record
- FD01 Email (required, up to 254 characters), e.g. "ana@example.com". How the reader signs in and can be reached
- FD02 Password (required, 8 to 128 characters), e.g. "correct-horse-battery". The secret that protects the reader's list

## Forms
- FM01 Book form: FD03, FD04, FD05

## New assumptions
- A03 (presentation, affects F04): Books are listed newest first; there is no manual ordering.
"
`;

exports[`render > scope reads as a short spec page 1`] = `
"# Shelf: scope

Shelf is a reading list for one reader at a time. You add the books you mean to read, keep each on a shelf that says where you are with it, and find them again by title or author. Nothing else gets in the way.

Audience: People who read a lot and lose track of what they wanted to read next.

Kinds of app: tracker

## Roles
- Reader (reader, signs in): Keeps a private reading list.

## Vocabulary
- Book (owned by Reader): A book the reader wants to read, is reading or has finished.
- Shelf (owned by Reader): Where a book stands: to read, reading or finished.

## In scope
- Sign up and sign in with email and password
- Add a book with a title, an author and a shelf
- Browse the list and search it by title or author
- Delete a book

## Out of scope
- Cover images: File uploads are not supported in the MVP. Instead: The website shows the title and author as text.

## Assumptions
- A01 (scope): A reader keeps one list; there are no shared lists.
- A02 (behavior): A reader fixes a mistake by deleting the Book and adding it again; editing a Book comes after the MVP.

## Questions
- Q01: Should a reader be able to rate a finished book? Why: A rating adds a field and a place to show it. Default: No; the shelf alone says where the reader is with a book.
"
`;
